 "confidence": "medium",
 "studyDesign": "Cross-sectional Study",
 "primaryDomain": "Sports Medicine & Injury",
 "domains": ["Sports Medicine & Injury", "Brain Health & Psychology", "Rehabilitation & Return to Sport"],
 "audiences": ["Athletic Training", "Sports Medicine"],
 "sports": ["American Football"],
 "populations": ["Collegiate"],
 "evidenceSummary": "A cross-sectional electronic survey of athletic trainers and team physicians from 46 of 65 NCAA Division 1 Autonomy Five football programs (71% response) described concussion baseline testing, prevention and management protocols for the 2018 season. Nearly all programs measured baseline postural stability/balance (98%) and 87% used computerized neurocognitive testing, while only 61% assessed vestibular and/or ocular function. Of the 46 programs, 26% started light aerobic exercise the day after a concussion if symptoms were stable, 24% waited 48 hours, 11% waited until asymptomatic, 4% waited until baseline, and 35% decided case by case; about half recommended a prevention measure (omega-3 24%, helmet padding 22%, cervical collars 4%).",
 "screeningLimitations": "Data are descriptive only, from one season (2018), with 46 of 65 programs responding, per-question response rates of 45% to 71%, and no respondent demographics, so the findings cannot establish which protocols work or represent all NCAA programs. The authors state that the survey does not support conclusions about relationships between program practices and outcomes.",
 "synthesisCaution": "Staff could read the most common practices (universal balance testing, or early aerobic exercise within 48 hours) as an accepted standard of care or as evidence that they work, when the survey only records what 46 programs reported doing in 2018.",
 "paper": {
   "year": 2024,
   "citation": "Clugston JR, Diemer K, Chrabaszcz SL, et al. What are the Protocols and Resources for Sport-Related Concussion Among Top National Collegiate Athletic Association Football Programs? A Cross-Sectional Survey of A5 Schools. Clin J Sport Med. 2024;00:1-7.",
   "doi": "10.1097/JSM.0000000000001241",
   "abstract": "This cross-sectional survey of NCAA Division 1 Autonomy Five football programs describes sport-related concussion protocols for the 2018 season, collected from athletic trainers and team physicians before a June 2019 NFL-NCAA football safety meeting. Of 46 responding programs, nearly all measured baseline balance, most used neurocognitive testing, and fewer than two-thirds assessed vestibular and/or ocular function at baseline. Prevention practices and the timing of post-concussion aerobic exercise varied widely across programs. The authors conclude that protocols are not standardized and that the findings are descriptive.",
   "tldr": "Baseline balance and neurocognitive testing are near-universal among top collegiate football programs, but vestibular and ocular testing, prevention practices and the timing of post-concussion exercise vary widely. Use these figures to benchmark a local protocol, not as proof that any one approach works.",
   "methods": "Electronic surveys with 16 concussion-related questions were sent to athletic trainers and team physicians at the 65 A5 football programs; 46 programs responded, with one aggregate response allowed per institution. Responses covered baseline assessment, prevention and management for the 2018 season, and were analyzed with descriptive statistics only.",
   "findings": "Baseline postural stability/balance was assessed by 98% of programs and computerized neurocognitive testing by 87%, while 61% assessed vestibular and/or ocular function. For prevention, 51% of programs recommended no additional measures; 24% recommended omega-3 supplements, 22% external helmet padding and 4% cervical compression collars. After concussion, 26% began light aerobic exercise the day after if symptoms were stable, 24% waited 48 hours, 11% waited until the athlete was asymptomatic, 4% waited until baseline, and 35% decided case by case.",
   "limitations": "The survey reflects the 2018 season and not current practice, and the 46 responding programs may not represent all A5 schools. Response rates varied by question (45% to 71%), respondent demographics were unavailable, and multi-select questions make cross-program comparison harder. The abstract says most institutions started light aerobic exercise within 48 hours, but the results show 50% did (26% on day 1 plus 24% at 48 hours) and the discussion says only half did; the results figures are used here.",
   "practicalImplications": "Programs can compare their baseline battery with these A5 figures, and the authors advise broader vestibular and ocular baseline testing, which 39% of programs did not perform. The paper states the evidence does not support omega-3 supplementation or cervical compression collars for concussion prevention, and only limited support exists for helmet padding, so these should not be adopted on the strength of this survey.",
   "athleteDev": "Performance Application: This is not a performance study. The transferable point is the baseline screening menu: balance and neurocognitive tests are common, while vestibular and ocular screens are used by about 61% of programs, and these are concussion baselines rather than training measures.",
   "rtp": "Return to Sport Application: The study describes post-concussion aerobic exercise timing but does not test outcomes, so it cannot show which timing returns athletes to play safely or sooner. It can support checking that a local protocol is written down and matches the consensus timing the authors cite (subthreshold activity on day 1 and controlled aerobic exercise within 2 days)."
 }
}

